            [
              "Anti-Kuominchun Right Column",
              "T'ien Wei-ch'in 田維勳",
              "2 Brigades Honanese, supported by new recruits. Credited with 20,000 men. At Sanchiatien, Mentoukou, Chaitang."
            ],
            [
              "Anti-Kuominchun Centre Column (5th Honan Division)",
              "Wang Wei-wei 王維尉",
              "Base at Kaopeitien. Credited with 12,000 men."
            ],
            [
              "Anti-Kuominchun Left Column.",
              "Wei Yi-san 魏[?]三",
              "Base at Yichow. Including remnants of Kuo Sung-ling's force, totalling 8,000."
            ],
            [
              "Yi Chun (5 Brigades)",
              "Mi Chen-piao 米振標",
              "Peking garrison under Wang Huai-ching (2 Brigades); also supporting T'ien Wei-ch'in. Credited with 25,000 men."
            ],
            [
              "9th Frontier Division",
              "Tung Chen-kuo (actual command of Li Lo-pin & Wang Meng-pi)",
              "This unit was formerly Tuan Ch'i-jui's bodyguard. It was taken over by Chang Tsung-ch'ang in April and sent to Tehchow, Shantung but deserted to Marshal Wu and after \"reorganization\" at Changteh, Chihli, was ordered to Hunan. Estimated strength, 7,000."
            ]
          ]
        }
      ],
      "footnotes": [
        "(It must be understood that with the overthrow of Yueh Wei-chun in Honan, a very large proportion of his army of 200,000 men surrendered and that both men and officers are occupying their Honan posts under the nominal command of Wu Pei-fu and subject only to \"reorganization\". Of these no accurate record has yet been compiled by Marshal Wu's staff officers. It is estimated that 26,000 of Yueh Wei-chun's command escaped into Shensi under arms. Twenty-one thousand of the 3rd Kuominchun and 8,000 of the 2nd Kuominchun were absorbed in the neighbourhood of Peking at the end of April, 1926, by T'ien Wei-ch'in and his colleagues.)"
      ],
      "context": "Lists the anti-Kuominchun columns operating in Chihli under Wu Pei-fu, with their commanders, base locations and credited strengths.",
